WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.901 --> 00:00:05.336
In 2019,

00:00:05.369 --> 00:00:10.274
I was working from my laptop,
from a beautiful beach in Bali.

00:00:10.307 --> 00:00:12.977
Drinking water right out
of a fresh coconut

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:15.012
and enjoying the warm ocean breeze,

00:00:15.046 --> 00:00:17.481
and I'm thinking, meh.

00:00:18.182 --> 00:00:21.585
Something was still missing,
and I couldn’t put my finger on it.

00:00:22.186 --> 00:00:26.424
How could I be in this scenario
and not feel fulfilled?

00:00:27.324 --> 00:00:31.062
I worked from 12 countries
in 12 months that year.

00:00:31.395 --> 00:00:35.099
An opportunity that was once rare
is now more possible

00:00:35.132 --> 00:00:37.168
for more people than ever before.

00:00:38.302 --> 00:00:42.106
Spotify recently introduced their new
"work from anywhere" policy

00:00:42.139 --> 00:00:44.675
in which they're giving employees
a choice of working

00:00:44.675 --> 00:00:48.012
wherever they do their most focused
and creative thinking.

00:00:48.579 --> 00:00:52.149
Dropbox's new way of working
is called "Virtual first,"

00:00:52.149 --> 00:00:55.953
in which remote work will be
the primary experience for employees,

00:00:55.986 --> 00:00:59.890
saving in-person meetings
for special occasions.

00:00:59.924 --> 00:01:01.092
And that's great news

00:01:01.092 --> 00:01:05.096
because over 50 percent of people
that work remotely say

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:07.331
that if their current company
doesn't give them

00:01:07.364 --> 00:01:10.701
some type of remote work option long-term,

00:01:10.734 --> 00:01:13.671
they'll look for a job
at a company that does.

00:01:14.338 --> 00:01:16.040
Well, problem solved, right?

00:01:16.507 --> 00:01:21.846
Except what people are saying
and what they're doing is inconsistent.

00:01:21.879 --> 00:01:24.915
In fact, according
to a recent McKinsey study,

00:01:24.949 --> 00:01:29.153
workplace flexibility wasn't even one
of the top five reasons

00:01:29.186 --> 00:01:31.589
people quit their jobs in the past year.

00:01:32.289 --> 00:01:36.227
More freedom at work
doesn't mean more fulfillment.

00:01:37.328 --> 00:01:40.397
Let me take you back to the end of 2018.

00:01:41.065 --> 00:01:44.602
I'm two years into doing my job
as the director of talent development

00:01:44.635 --> 00:01:46.804
for a New York-based tech company.

00:01:46.837 --> 00:01:49.473
And I'm doing what I love to do the most,

00:01:49.507 --> 00:01:53.844
creating and launching employee
development programs from scratch.

00:01:54.378 --> 00:01:56.580
And then my boss says to me,

00:01:56.614 --> 00:01:59.150
"Next year is going to be
the year of maintenance,

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:03.721
focusing on refining existing programs
instead of launching new ones."

00:02:04.288 --> 00:02:06.357
I'm not a maintenance-mode person.

00:02:06.657 --> 00:02:10.494
While I understand its importance,
it's really not where I shine,

00:02:10.494 --> 00:02:12.897
so I wasn't looking forward
to the year ahead.

00:02:13.531 --> 00:02:17.368
Meanwhile, I was reading new research
on the future of work,

00:02:17.401 --> 00:02:21.872
and all signs were pointing
to a remote and hybrid-work future.

00:02:22.373 --> 00:02:26.610
We now know that the pandemic
accelerated that by at least a decade.

00:02:27.878 --> 00:02:30.181
I even found a work-and-travel program

00:02:30.214 --> 00:02:34.685
that coordinates a group of professionals
to work remotely from around the world

00:02:34.718 --> 00:02:39.089
with coworking spaces and living
accommodations in each city.

00:02:39.123 --> 00:02:41.125
I was immediately hooked.

00:02:41.158 --> 00:02:44.328
I had always wanted to travel
for a longer period of time,

00:02:44.361 --> 00:02:47.198
but I thought I'd have to quit my job
in order to do that.

00:02:48.532 --> 00:02:52.803
Then I thought, well, I could do
maintenance mode from anywhere.

00:02:53.103 --> 00:02:57.007
And given my current company's
global expansion plans,

00:02:57.041 --> 00:03:01.612
I knew I had to think about where current
and future employees could be located.

00:03:01.912 --> 00:03:05.282
If I could convince my company
that I should work remotely

00:03:05.316 --> 00:03:07.351
to better understand the opportunities

00:03:07.351 --> 00:03:10.221
and challenges that remote workers face,

00:03:10.254 --> 00:03:12.423
it could be a win-win for both of us.

00:03:12.756 --> 00:03:15.526
And a month later,
I ended my apartment lease,

00:03:15.559 --> 00:03:16.860
sold my furniture

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:21.365
and hopped on a one-way flight
from cold and snowy New York City

00:03:21.365 --> 00:03:22.533
to Lima, Peru,

00:03:22.566 --> 00:03:25.569
to start my year-long trip with my cohort.

00:03:25.569 --> 00:03:26.737
And let me tell you,

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:29.373
those first few months were incredible.

00:03:30.307 --> 00:03:34.478
I attended a virtual team meeting
for my coworking space at Lima

00:03:34.478 --> 00:03:38.349
before heading out to go surfing
at sunset on a regular Tuesday.

00:03:38.882 --> 00:03:41.485
I launched a work program
from inside of a pool

00:03:41.485 --> 00:03:43.587
in Puerto Escondido, Mexico.

00:03:44.154 --> 00:03:48.058
And I negotiated a contract
from a cafe in Hanoi, Vietnam,

00:03:48.092 --> 00:03:52.096
before jumping on the back of a motorbike
to go eat bun cha with friends.

00:03:52.596 --> 00:03:56.433
I met remote workers and digital
nomads from around the world.

00:03:56.734 --> 00:04:00.271
Each of us had a different story
of how we got there.

00:04:00.304 --> 00:04:04.408
Some had just been divorced
or gotten out of long-term relationships

00:04:04.441 --> 00:04:07.044
and convinced their employers
to let them work remotely

00:04:07.044 --> 00:04:08.612
so that they could get away.

00:04:08.946 --> 00:04:12.216
There were three corporate lawyers
who all hated their jobs

00:04:12.249 --> 00:04:15.619
and thought that doing them from a beach
would make it more tolerable.

00:04:15.919 --> 00:04:20.958
And some just wanted to be as far away
from micromanagement and office politics

00:04:20.991 --> 00:04:22.359
as possible.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:25.996
But still, after a few months,

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:30.134
when that honeymoon period was over
and the novelty faded,

00:04:30.134 --> 00:04:35.339
we realized that traveling was just
a Band-Aid solution for the real problems.

00:04:35.939 --> 00:04:40.377
I wondered, how could we get
this "work from anywhere" life right?

00:04:40.411 --> 00:04:42.579
So I interviewed dozens of remote workers,

00:04:42.613 --> 00:04:45.616
I conducted a focus group,
and I surveyed hundreds of people.

00:04:45.649 --> 00:04:47.985
And you know what I saw in all that data?

00:04:48.018 --> 00:04:50.788
Wherever you go, there you are.

00:04:51.322 --> 00:04:54.958
If you hate your job in Houston,
you will hate it in Hanoi.

00:04:55.259 --> 00:04:59.830
What really needs fixing
is your motivation to do the work itself.

00:04:59.863 --> 00:05:02.333
We believe we need
a certain amount of money

00:05:02.366 --> 00:05:05.769
to create or maintain
the lifestyle that we've envisioned.

00:05:06.203 --> 00:05:09.840
We think we need a specific job title
that aligns with the identity

00:05:09.873 --> 00:05:13.243
that we or our parents imagined
for us a decade ago.

00:05:13.711 --> 00:05:15.546
And we don't realize that we get stuck

00:05:15.546 --> 00:05:18.649
in the motions of work routines
without intention.

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:22.252
Minimizing these pressures
around your money,

00:05:22.252 --> 00:05:24.321
identity and routines

00:05:24.355 --> 00:05:28.726
and focusing on opportunities
for more growth, impact and joy

00:05:28.726 --> 00:05:33.030
is the key to a more fulfilling career
no matter where you're located.

00:05:34.031 --> 00:05:37.334
And the good news is that you might
not have to overhaul your career

00:05:37.368 --> 00:05:39.269
or life to feel fulfilled.

00:05:40.003 --> 00:05:43.941
Esther Perel, world-renowned
psychotherapist and couples counselor,

00:05:43.974 --> 00:05:46.276
says that in the West today,

00:05:46.276 --> 00:05:51.281
most of us are going to have two or three
marriages or committed relationships,

00:05:51.315 --> 00:05:54.885
and some of us are going to do it
with the same person.

00:05:55.753 --> 00:05:57.654
Think about that for a second.

00:05:57.654 --> 00:06:02.960
She's saying that some of us will build
and experience a new relationship

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:04.661
with the same person.

00:06:05.496 --> 00:06:09.233
Perhaps something similar could be said
about your relationship to your work

00:06:09.233 --> 00:06:10.968
at the same company.

00:06:12.202 --> 00:06:16.573
Perhaps after a challenging time,
an enticing external offer

00:06:16.573 --> 00:06:18.909
or having been together for a while,

00:06:18.942 --> 00:06:20.344
instead of breaking up,

00:06:20.377 --> 00:06:23.747
you could find and create
a better path forward together.

00:06:24.748 --> 00:06:27.518
And I'm not saying you're going to get
everything you want

00:06:27.518 --> 00:06:28.919
out of your current job,

00:06:28.952 --> 00:06:31.422
but given how much time
you spend working,

00:06:31.455 --> 00:06:34.558
isn't it worth a shot to make it
as fulfilling as possible?

00:06:35.426 --> 00:06:38.796
When the pandemic hit
and much of the world went remote,

00:06:38.796 --> 00:06:40.764
I saw my corporate friends in the States

00:06:40.798 --> 00:06:43.834
follow the exact same trajectory
that we did abroad.

00:06:44.334 --> 00:06:46.670
The thrill of working home
in your pajamas,

00:06:46.703 --> 00:06:49.039
having more flexibility
with your schedule,

00:06:49.039 --> 00:06:51.742
being able to do the laundry
in between meetings,

00:06:51.775 --> 00:06:55.612
all of which can make a real difference
in improving your daily life.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:07:00.517
But still, once the novelty faded
and the reality set in,

00:07:00.551 --> 00:07:03.420
people had to face whatever
underlying issues they'd had

00:07:03.454 --> 00:07:05.222
before the pandemic started.

00:07:05.956 --> 00:07:08.725
When the perks of an office job
were taken away,

00:07:08.759 --> 00:07:11.195
whether it was the free snacks
in the break room,

00:07:11.195 --> 00:07:14.198
the water-cooler conversation
with colleagues,

00:07:14.231 --> 00:07:17.367
what people were left with
was the work itself

00:07:17.401 --> 00:07:20.404
and their motivation,
or lack thereof, to do it.

00:07:21.205 --> 00:07:24.608
And they began to ask themselves
really important questions:

00:07:24.608 --> 00:07:26.610
Do I even like my job?

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:28.679
Is this really worth my time?

00:07:28.712 --> 00:07:30.481
Is this what I want to be doing?

00:07:30.881 --> 00:07:33.550
And these are great questions to ask.

00:07:33.584 --> 00:07:35.819
The problem is, for way too many people,

00:07:35.819 --> 00:07:39.323
they thought they had to completely
overhaul their career or life.

00:07:40.123 --> 00:07:44.094
More people voluntarily
quit their jobs in 2021

00:07:44.127 --> 00:07:46.930
than any other year in recorded history.

00:07:47.965 --> 00:07:51.735
They wanted a fresh start and a new
beginning after a challenging time.

00:07:52.269 --> 00:07:53.637
Here's the kicker, though.

00:07:54.638 --> 00:07:58.208
Almost 50 percent of the people
that did find jobs after quitting

00:07:58.242 --> 00:08:02.913
say that their new job has not lived up
to their expectations.

00:08:03.647 --> 00:08:08.519
They found out, just like we did
in Peru, Mexico and Vietnam,

00:08:08.519 --> 00:08:11.121
that wherever you go, there you are.

00:08:12.356 --> 00:08:14.958
Perhaps you could start
the next chapter of your career

00:08:14.958 --> 00:08:16.693
in an organization that knows you,

00:08:16.727 --> 00:08:20.163
where you've built credibility
and trust and a network,

00:08:20.163 --> 00:08:23.800
and where they'd be willing to let you
try things you haven't done before.

00:08:24.368 --> 00:08:27.471
Now, I'm not saying you should stay
in a toxic work environment.

00:08:27.471 --> 00:08:30.007
And sometimes you do need
a larger-scale change,

00:08:30.007 --> 00:08:32.209
like a new job or a new career.

00:08:32.843 --> 00:08:37.180
But before you make a decision
that you think will make you happier,

00:08:37.214 --> 00:08:41.718
I want you to consider that happiness
is a feeling that doesn't last.

00:08:42.319 --> 00:08:46.523
It often is what we want in the moment
and depends on external circumstances.

00:08:47.057 --> 00:08:50.527
What does last, however, is fulfillment.

00:08:50.961 --> 00:08:55.232
Fulfillment is richer,
deeper and longer-term

00:08:55.265 --> 00:08:59.102
and often takes a longer amount of time
and intention to get it.

00:09:00.404 --> 00:09:02.973
As you look to generate fulfillment,

00:09:03.006 --> 00:09:07.678
I want you to consider three questions
tied to three of its key components

00:09:07.711 --> 00:09:09.813
that are more internally driven.

00:09:10.681 --> 00:09:12.215
The first is joy.

00:09:12.849 --> 00:09:16.453
What kind of work energizes you
that you'd love to do more of?

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:20.157
Now, I want you to remember that just
because you're good at something,

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:22.593
it doesn't mean that you enjoy doing it.

00:09:22.593 --> 00:09:26.530
So really consider what gives you
a deep feeling of contentment.

00:09:27.364 --> 00:09:29.232
Next is impact.

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:33.103
How could you provide more value
to your company or clients

00:09:33.136 --> 00:09:34.972
by doing this kind of work?

00:09:35.572 --> 00:09:39.142
And if you're having trouble answering
that question, then ask your manager,

00:09:39.176 --> 00:09:41.378
your peers or clients directly.

00:09:41.778 --> 00:09:44.381
Focus on work that's in that sweet spot

00:09:44.414 --> 00:09:48.018
of meeting both your interests
and the client's needs

00:09:48.051 --> 00:09:49.553
or the business needs.

00:09:50.387 --> 00:09:52.422
And finally, growth.

00:09:52.956 --> 00:09:57.594
Which knowledge or skills could you
develop through these experiences?

00:09:57.594 --> 00:10:00.797
Feeling a sense of progress
toward meaningful work

00:10:00.797 --> 00:10:03.533
is one of the biggest
drivers of fulfillment.

00:10:04.568 --> 00:10:08.472
I asked myself these same three questions
when I was feeling happy

00:10:08.505 --> 00:10:11.975
yet not fulfilled with my life
working from a beach.

00:10:12.776 --> 00:10:16.613
And I found out that I find
joy in interviewing,

00:10:16.647 --> 00:10:20.217
researching and talking to remote
workers about their lives.

00:10:20.784 --> 00:10:23.987
I made a significant impact
by facilitating workshops

00:10:24.021 --> 00:10:27.557
to help them develop
their skills and their careers.

00:10:27.591 --> 00:10:30.594
And growth came in the form
of me figuring out

00:10:30.594 --> 00:10:35.799
how to distill a career's worth
of the best HR advice and expertise

00:10:35.799 --> 00:10:39.102
into an easily digestible book
for remote workers.

00:10:40.837 --> 00:10:43.206
Thousands of organizations
around the world

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:45.475
are finally understanding the importance

00:10:45.509 --> 00:10:48.211
of giving employees a choice
of where to work.

00:10:48.745 --> 00:10:51.882
And while being given that choice
is certainly meaningful,

00:10:51.915 --> 00:10:53.583
what’s even more empowering

00:10:53.617 --> 00:10:56.553
is the fulfillment that comes
from doing work

00:10:56.553 --> 00:10:59.656
that truly lights you up,
wherever you are.

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:01.558
Thank you.

